
====================================================================================================
S1. Verify Dodelson's claim on the VERIFIED DESI fits: is rho_DE increasing with time?
====================================================================================================
  rho_DE increases with cosmic time exactly when 1+w < 0 (phantom), since
      d rho_DE/dt = -3 H (1+w) rho_DE.
  For CPL, w -> w0 + wa as z -> infinity, so the high-z behaviour is set by w0+wa.
  combination              w0+wa    w(0)    w(1)    w(3)  z_cross(w=-1)  rho(3)/rho(0)
  LCDM (null)             -1.000  -1.000  -1.000  -1.000            n/a          1.000
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+       -1.438  -0.858  -1.148  -1.293          0.324          0.597
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie     -1.551  -0.821  -1.186  -1.369          0.325          0.523
  DR2+CMB+Union3          -1.812  -0.662  -1.237  -1.524          0.416          0.454
  DR2+CMB (no SN)         -2.170  -0.420  -1.295  -1.732          0.496          0.395
  READ: every SN-inclusive fit has w0+wa < -1, i.e. w(z) crosses BELOW -1 at modest z and the
  dark-energy density was SMALLER in the past -- it has been GROWING as the universe expands.
  rho_DE(z=3)/rho_DE(0) < 1 in all of them (0.60 down to 0.40), confirming Dodelson's statement.
  [OK] all 4 evolving fits are phantom at high z (w0+wa < -1) -- Dodelson's 'red line' is crossed by every one, verified from the fitted values rather than taken on his word

====================================================================================================
S2. What the framework INHERITS -- its prediction lives inside the disputed region
====================================================================================================
  a0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_Lambda)  =>  a0(z)/a0(0) = sqrt(rho_DE(z)/rho_DE(0))
  combination             a0(3)/a0(0) canon  a0 rising with time?
  LCDM (null)                         1.000                    no
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+                   0.772                   YES
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie                 0.723                   YES
  DR2+CMB+Union3                      0.674                   YES
  DR2+CMB (no SN)                     0.629                   YES
  So on the canonical footing the framework says a0 was LOWER in the past and has been
  RISING -- which is only true because rho_Lambda is rising, which is the very thing the
  field calls unphysical. THE FRAMEWORK'S DISTINCTIVE COSMOLOGICAL PREDICTION IS A RIDER ON
  THE DISPUTED FEATURE. Two consequences, both important and neither comfortable:
   (a) If the field retreats from phantom DE -- via a non-phantom parametrization, or the
       'ignore the most uncertain sliver of data' route Dodelson flags -- then (w0,wa) moves
       back toward (-1, 0), rho_Lambda goes constant, and the framework's a0(z) signal goes
       to ZERO. The prediction is not just hostage to DESI; it is hostage to a MODELLING
       CHOICE the field is actively uncomfortable with.
   (b) The framework cannot claim the phantom region as a success while the field treats it
       as a symptom. Riding it both ways is not available.
  [OK] canonical a0 was LOWER in the past for the DES-Dovekie fit, i.e. the framework requires the growing-rho_Lambda behaviour the field disputes

====================================================================================================
S3. *** THE CIRCULARITY -- stated against interest, because it undercuts my own ledger ***
====================================================================================================
  The (w0, wa) values the framework consumes were inferred by fitting SN and BAO distances
  with STANDARD inertia and standard growth. The framework asserts inertia is modified at
  accelerations near a0. So feeding those (w0, wa) into a0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_Lambda) uses,
  as input, a number derived under an assumption the framework denies.
  This is a real logical defect in the crispy-dark-matter ledger built earlier today
  (mi_crispy_dark_matter_ledger_2026.py): that document treats DESI's (w0, wa) as an
  independent measurement feeding a parameter-free a0(z). It is only independent if modified
  inertia does not affect the distance-redshift relation. That is an ASSUMPTION, not a
  result, and it is nowhere established in the corpus.
  It does NOT invalidate the ledger's two structural findings (the DESI-dependent signal is
  small and sign-changing; the alt footing is empty) -- those are shape statements. It does
  mean the ledger's 'parameter-free' claim is conditional on an unbuilt result.
  [OK] the circularity is recorded as a defect in my own prior output, not buried

====================================================================================================
S4. THE OPENING -- is the expansion even in the regime where MI could matter?
====================================================================================================
  The framework's own coincidence is that the cosmic acceleration scale and a0 are the same
  order. Make that quantitative rather than rhetorical:
    canonical  a0 = 9.360e-11,  c H0 = 6.548e-10 m/s^2,  ratio c H0 / a0 =   7.00
    alt        a0 = 1.130e-10,  c H0 = 6.548e-10 m/s^2,  ratio c H0 / a0 =   5.79
  [OK] c H0 / a0 = 7.00 -- the same order. Cosmological dynamics is NOT deep in the Newtonian regime with respect to a0, so MI cannot be assumed irrelevant to the expansion history without a calculation

  Now the size of the target. How big is the phantom signal in the observable SNe actually
  measure -- distance modulus? (Om and H0 held fixed; pure w(z) effect.)
  combination                z=0.2     z=0.5     z=1.0     z=1.5     z=2.0
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+        -0.0198   -0.0246   -0.0161   -0.0084   -0.0039
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie      -0.0250   -0.0311   -0.0205   -0.0108   -0.0052
  DR2+CMB+Union3           -0.0499   -0.0690   -0.0567   -0.0410   -0.0307
  DR2+CMB (no SN)          -0.0891   -0.1309   -0.1187   -0.0935   -0.0757
  (Delta distance modulus in magnitudes, evolving-DE minus LCDM)
  Peak |Delta mu| across all fits and redshifts: 0.1309 mag
  [OK] across the SN-INCLUSIVE fits the whole phantom signal peaks at 0.069 mag -- a few hundredths of a magnitude (the BAO+CMB-only fit reaches 0.131, but it has no SNe and the widest contours)

  *** THE SHARPEST POINT IN THIS SCRIPT, and it is a caution, not an encouragement. ***
  The SN-inclusive signal is 0.069 mag at peak. Type Ia intrinsic scatter is ~0.15 mag
  PER SUPERNOVA, so this is a sub-scatter tilt extracted statistically from thousands of
  objects. That makes it exactly the size of the CALIBRATION SYSTEMATICS currently being
  revised: the DES-Dovekie recalibration alone moved that sample from 4.2 sigma to 3.2-3.4,
  and Pantheon+ moved the other way (2.8 -> 3.2). A 0.8-sigma swing from a recalibration on a
  signal this small means the systematic and the signal are COMPARABLE IN SIZE.
  Two honest consequences:
   (a) FOR the artifact idea: a mechanism only needs to tilt distances by ~0.03 mag. Low bar.
   (b) AGAINST relying on it: so does any unmodelled calibration drift. The framework should
       NOT stake a claim on a signal that photometric recalibrations are still moving at the
       same amplitude. Dodelson's second point -- that ignoring the most uncertain sliver of
       data restores the cosmological constant -- is the same warning from the data side.
  THIS IS THE OPENING, AND IT IS A GENUINE ONE. The signal that has the field 'groping in
  the dark' (Dodelson) is a few-hundredths-of-a-magnitude tilt in the distance-redshift
  relation. It does NOT require a substance with growing energy density -- it requires
  anything that tilts distances by ~0.03-0.10 mag over 0 < z < 2. A modified-inertia
  cosmology operating at c H0 / a0 ~ 7 is a candidate for producing such a tilt with
  Lambda held CONSTANT -- which would explain the anomaly WITHOUT crossing Dodelson's red
  line, and would keep a0 constant, which is what the low-z RAR prefers anyway.

====================================================================================================
S5. VERDICT -- live door, and the one calculation that decides it
====================================================================================================
  THE IDEA, stated so it can be killed: DESI+SN may be seeing an APPARENT phantom crossing
  that is an ARTIFACT of fitting a modified-inertia universe with standard-inertia distance
  relations. If so, Lambda is constant, a0 is constant, and the 'unphysical' growing-rho_DE
  is a fitting artifact rather than a substance.
  WHY IT IS WORTH TAKING SERIOUSLY (all computed above, none rhetorical):
   1. Target size is small: 0.131 mag peak. Not a huge effect to manufacture.
   2. c H0 / a0 = 7.00: the expansion is at the a0 scale, so MI is not a priori
      negligible. This is the framework's own central coincidence, used as a lever.
   3. It would REMOVE the framework's dependence on the disputed phantom region (S2) and
      REMOVE the circularity in my own ledger (S3) -- both problems solved by the same move.
   4. It answers Dodelson's actual question ('I still don't see the end game') with a
      mechanism rather than another parametrization, which is what he says is missing.

  WHY IT IS NOT A RESULT YET, stated plainly:
   * The MI linear cosmology DOES NOT EXIST. Without it, the apparent (w0, wa) that an MI
     universe would fake CANNOT be computed. Everything above establishes that the target is
     the right SIZE and the right REGIME -- not that MI hits it, and not the SIGN.
   * The sign is the immediate risk: MI could just as easily tilt distances the WRONG way,
     making the anomaly worse. Nothing computed here fixes the sign.
   * The corpus's own prerequisite is now met, though: the off-circular closure was narrowed
     570% -> 7.9% (mi_offcircular_closure_collapse_2026), which was the stated blocker on
     building the MI linear cosmology. So the blocking item is cleared.

  THE ONE BOUNDED CALCULATION: derive the MI modified Friedmann/growth pair from the v4
  action, compute d_L(z), fit it with standard w0waCDM, and read off the apparent (w0, wa).
  Success looks like landing inside the DESI contours with Lambda constant. Failure looks
   like the wrong sign or an effect << 0.131 mag. Both are publishable; the second
  would close the framework's most distinctive cosmological hope, which is worth knowing.

  NOTE ON PRIOR ART -- CHECK BEFORE BUILDING. 'Apparent dark-energy evolution as an artifact
  of a modified gravity/inertia fit' is an obvious idea and the literature on
  backreaction, inhomogeneous cosmology (Buchert), timescape (Wiltshire), and MOND-cosmology
  fits is large. The CMB lane of this corpus was already retired once for prior art
  (Gnedin 2008, arXiv:0809.2790). A literature check is the FIRST step here, not the last.
